/**
\page javascript_mvc_guide JavaScript application framework

The MVC interface inventory is generated separately in `pdg-mvc-js.h` and
`pdg-mvc-js.json`. The core engine inventory remains in `pdg-js.h` and
`pdg-js.json`. Run `tools/make-idl-javascript.sh` to regenerate both sets.

The optional MVC module provides Application, Controller, View, and controls such
as Button, Dialog, ListBox, and EditText. It is separate from the main \c pdg
export: importing \c pdg does not make \c pdg.View or \c pdg.Controller available.
See \ref javascript_mvc_inventory for the exports and their own public members.

\note API stability: Evolving. Rendering requires a graphics-capable runtime.
The standard Node addon does not provide graphics merely because its package
contains the JavaScript framework sources.

\section mvc_imports Importing the framework

In the repository control gallery, located under \c test/js, the import is:
\code{.js}
const pdg = require('pdg');
const framework = require('../../src/js/mvc-app');
const { Controller } = framework.Controller;
const { View } = framework.View;
\endcode

Several index exports are module objects: for example, the View constructor is
\c framework.View.View, not \c framework.View. The inventory preserves these
nested paths.

The relative path is specific to that example's location. Adjust it for your
application. The Node package copies these sources under \c lib/mvc-app, where
the package import is \c require('pdg/lib/mvc-app'). That path does not enable
GUI support in a headless runtime. Browser and iOS applications must include the
framework files in their existing module loader; neither an ES module entry nor
a \c pdg/mvc alias is implied by this documentation.

\section mvc_view_lifecycle Creating and using a View

View extends AnimatedAttributes and inherits its animation and appearance
operations. It has no physics body. Its constructor is
\c new\ View(controller,rect,binding), with binding defaulting to zero.
The controller is required, and construction registers the View with it.

Given an existing controller attached to an application and graphics port:
\code{.js}
const view = new View(controller, new pdg.Rect(0, 0, 200, 100));
view.moveTo(150, 80, 0.5);
view.changeFillOpacity(0.5, 0.25);
\endcode

This creates a base View; subclass drawSelf() or use a concrete control to provide
visible content. The controller advances view animations during its draw cycle.
Do not advance registered views a second time manually. Durations are seconds and
rotation is clockwise radians. View layout size is independent of scale, and its
location is the center of the unrotated layout rectangle.

Use \c view.setParentView(parent) for a visual parent, and \c null to detach.
The link supplies inherited transforms, clipping, and visibility; it does not
replace controller ownership. Nested views must belong to the same controller.
\c view.getParentView() returns a View or null. \c view.getController(Type)
returns the controller only when it is an instance of Type, otherwise null.

\section mvc_inventory_scope Inventory coverage

The generated inventory enumerates module exports, nested module exports, and own prototype/static
members without constructing controls or invoking getters. Inherited operations
are identified through the base class. It deliberately labels unresolved method
signatures and does not infer instance fields from a dummy constructor call.
Complete typed MVC signatures remain separate work; this inventory establishes
coverage without inventing overloads or requiring a live application window.

\see AnimatedAttributes, \ref javascript_mvc_inventory
*/
